/* ── wp-admin ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The admin pages themselves — the screens around the editor, not the
   editable area inside it. main.css and editor.pcss both go in through
   add_editor_style(), which puts them inside the canvas, so neither can
   reach the furniture that surrounds it. This is loaded on the admin page.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* The classic editor shows fields, not content.
   A page built from section blocks keeps its content as block markup, edited
   in the block editor and nowhere else. The classic view is opened for the
   field groups beside it, so the content box is hidden: what cannot be
   reached cannot be changed by hand.

   Hidden, not removed from the form — the field keeps posting what it was
   given, so a save carries the markup back unchanged and autosave keeps
   working on it. #postdivrich is the classic editor's own wrapper and exists
   on no other screen. ACF's own wysiwyg fields are separate elements and are
   left alone: those are what the classic view is for. */
#postdivrich {
  display: none;
}
